Use sha2's portable SHA-256 kernels instead of the `asm` ones.

The `asm` feature pulls in sha2-asm, whose ARMv8 crypto-extension kernels
require the compiler to target a CPU with those extensions. BinaryBuilder
forbids forcing an arch via -march, so they cannot be assembled in this
environment.

Dropping the feature costs throughput on aarch64 only. x86_64 is unaffected
in practice: the portable implementation still detects SHA-NI at runtime.

--- a/crates/infrastore-core/Cargo.toml
+++ b/crates/infrastore-core/Cargo.toml
@@ -41,7 +41,7 @@
 # Apple Silicon. sha2-asm does not build under MSVC, so Windows MSVC keeps the
 # portable implementation (x86_64 still auto-detects SHA-NI at runtime).
 [target.'cfg(not(target_env = "msvc"))'.dependencies]
-sha2 = { workspace = true, features = ["asm"] }
+sha2 = { workspace = true }
 
 [target.'cfg(target_env = "msvc")'.dependencies]
 sha2 = { workspace = true }
